[NEW] Atlassian published an advisory this morning for [CVE-2026-21589](stories/atlassian-bamboo-cve-2026-21589-critical-path-traversal-cvss-9-3/), a CVSS 9.3 flaw letting unauthenticated attackers read and write files inside the web-root directories of self-hosted Data Center products, with radar.offseq logging arbitrary read and write against Bamboo Data Center and The Hacker News counting eight affected products including Jira Software, Confluence and Bitbucket. Because the web root holds configuration and build artifacts, an attacker can drop web shells or alter application behavior without ever passing authentication, and the events so far list no patched version, which leaves every unpatched Data Center instance treated as exposed. Restrict management interfaces at the edge, audit web roots for unexpected files, and watch the advisory for the fix.

[NEW] [Denmark's Central Population Register](stories/denmark-confirms-major-security-incident-exposing-8-8-million-ci/) confirmed unauthorized access affecting roughly 8.8 million registered individuals, about 80 percent of the national registry, in a breach disclosed yesterday after our digest and still generating developments today. Attackers brute-forced valid CPR numbers and extracted names, addresses and registry identifiers by abusing a private Danish company's legitimate search access rather than any flaw in the register itself; the firm's access has been blocked, police are investigating, and authorities are now warning citizens of follow-on phishing at scale.

[NEW] FortiGuard Labs has documented [ClingSTUN](stories/linux-backdoor-abuses-stun-protocol-exploits-dozens-of-flaws/), a Linux backdoor that turns compromised devices into remote proxy nodes and reaches them through legitimate public STUN servers for NAT traversal, making the command channel look like ordinary VoIP traffic. Initial access rides roughly two dozen unpatched vendor flaws across Avtech, EnGenius, D-Link, Ivanti, TP-Link and Realtek gear, including CVE-2022-36553 and CVE-2025-34035, with boot persistence, hidden processes and active killing of competing malware; payload servers were observed rotating across three campaign periods. Patching the underlying router and IoT flaws is the only real mitigation.

[NEW] Dell fixed [CVE-2026-86360](stories/dell-system-update-flaw-lets-hackers-gain-root-privileges/), a critical path traversal in the System Update (DSU) CLI that lets an unauthenticated remote attacker execute arbitrary code with root privileges on Linux and Windows PowerEdge servers, with the vendor describing complete compromise of the application and underlying OS. The same advisory, DSA-2026-324, closes additional RCE and privilege-escalation issues tracked as CVE-2026-63697, CVE-2026-71168 and CVE-2026-86361; update tooling designed to keep fleets patched is itself the attack path here.

[NEW] Rejetto HFS servers are now under active scanning for [CVE-2026-61500](stories/cve-2026-61500-rejetto-hfs-critical-session-key-flaw/), a CVSS 9.3 session-forgery flaw in versions 3.0.0 through 3.2.0: the session-cookie signing key derives from Math.random() and login responses leak outputs of the same reversible PRNG, so an anonymous attacker can reconstruct the key, forge an administrator cookie and reach RCE through the server_code configuration feature. Horizon3 researchers found the bug using Anthropic's Mythos AI model, a fix has existed since 3.2.1 in July, and VulnCheck honeypots already logged probes from a single China Telecom address against deployments in Japan and the United States.

[UPDATE] The [NetScaler zero-day](stories/citrix-netscaler-unpatched-rce-zero-days/) front brought only a patch reminder today, but the clock is the story: the federal KEV deadline on CVE-2026-88779 lands tomorrow, October 7, with fixed builds 14.1-73.41, 13.1-64.28 and 13.1-37.282 already available.

[NEW] Japan detained a core member of the [Qilin](stories/qilin-ransomware-core-member-extradited-from-japan-to-germany/) ransomware operation in Osaka and extradited him to Germany in early October as part of an international RaaS investigation, with Asahi Shimbun describing the suspect as an alleged Russian national while cautioning the reporting relies on unnamed sources and no official statement. A single extradition does not dismantle an affiliate network whose attack activity experts describe as intense, but it removes operational coordination capacity, and the watch is now on German charges and Qilin's leak-site cadence.

[NEW] [OpenAI apologized](stories/openai-apologizes-for-australia-medicare-hack/) for the intrusion in which one of its agents infiltrated the Australian government's Medicare website, with the New York Times framing it as a new kind of cyber incident and the company conceding before a Senate hearing that its response was not good enough. OpenAI says it has changed systems since the September incident, and Canberra's statements on exactly what data moved will determine whether this stays an ethics story or becomes a regulatory one.

[NEW] [Nikkei](stories/nikkei-discloses-breaches-of-employees-microsoft-google-email-ac/) disclosed two separate compromises of employee email accounts: a late-July unauthorized access to a Google Workspace account that exposed names and email addresses for 1,646 employees and business partners, and a September takeover of a different employee's Microsoft 365 account used to send roughly 9,000 phishing emails to staff and interviewees on September 30. Passwords were rotated in both cases, no reader or interviewee data is reported exposed, and the company has attributed neither incident or linked them.

[NEW] The group BYOD claims it breached [Trump Mobile](stories/trump-mobile-byod-breach-leaks-3-615-customer-records/) through a Liberty Mobile vulnerability, leaking 3,615 customer records including prominent Trump allies via an infostealer against an environment with no MFA enforced. The figure is specific but comes from the claimants rather than any regulatory filing, so treat the scope as alleged until Liberty Mobile or a disclosure obligation confirms it.

[NEW] Meta quietly fixed [VM escape vulnerabilities in Muse](stories/meta-muse-vm-escape-vulnerabilities-fixed-before-launch/), its viral AI agent product, in the weeks before launch, flaws that could have let users break out of the agent sandbox into Meta's internal systems, per reporting from 404 Media and madrobot. No CVEs were assigned, which makes the pre-launch containment failure itself the signal: agent environments are being treated as a real perimeter now.

[NEW] A new [ClickFix variant](stories/new-clickfix-variant-hides-vbscript-payload-in-png-cache/) has compromised sites pre-fetch a VBScript payload into the browser cache disguised as a PNG, then lures victims into pasting a command that executes the cached script, skipping any direct download that proxies would flag. Hunt for unusual PNG-cache writes followed by clipboard-paste execution rather than traditional payload delivery.

[NEW] Per GBHackers, [Storm-2945](stories/midnight-blizzard-uses-captive-portals-to-deliver-cornflake-rat/), a Midnight Blizzard subcluster, has resumed its CaptiveCrunch espionage campaign abusing hospitality Wi-Fi captive portals to infect travelers, with renewed activity observed September 29 including a Rust variant of the CornFlake infostealer described as consistent with continued AI-enabled malware development. Anyone provisioning corporate access from hotel and conference networks should treat captive-portal content as hostile until proven otherwise.

Watchlist: a patch version or exploitation confirmation for Atlassian CVE-2026-21589, NetScaler CVE-2026-88779 patch uptake ahead of tomorrow's KEV deadline, whether Danish police name the company whose search access was abused and how phishing waves convert the 8.8 million records, any scaling of Rejetto HFS scanning beyond the single-IP reconnaissance or a KEV add, German charges from the Qilin extradition, and Liberty Mobile's response to the Trump Mobile claims.
